<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Nicholas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th century</span> · Also known as Nicholas of Myra, Nicholas of Bari</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Nicholas was a 4th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myra">Myra</a> in Lycia, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, venerated across Eastern and Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> as a wonder-worker and patron of sailors and children. In 1087 merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a> carried his relics from Myra to their city, where the Basilica of Saint Nicholas was built to house them. The shrine made Bari one of the great pilgrimage centres of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas.jpg" alt="Saint Nicholas" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jaroslav Čermák · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaroslav_%C4%8Cerm%C3%A1k_(1831_-_1878)_-_Sv._Mikul%C3%A1%C5%A1_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
